--- ANSWER ---
**Verdict: the claim's general idea, that the extra items come from wiring that later steps still owe, may be right. But the explanation as written is partly wrong, and nothing measured so far ties any of the 11 items to an owed action.**

## 1. Strongest reason the claim is wrong: its list of causes doesn't match the evidence

- **The frame index input (A48) can't account for any new item.** The step's own plan lists `#376 'frame index'` as already open in the P5-1 file (`tools/bench/plan_ring_p5s02a.json:78-82`). Whatever error it produces is already among the 45 base items.
- **The case tunnels it names don't exist yet, and the error wording points at loop tunnels.** `t_in3`, `t_gi`, `t_hi`, `t_go` and `t_ho` are tunnels on the new Case #31308, and they belong to step 2. An object that hasn't been created can't produce an Error List item.
  - The two new tunnel items both read **"Loop Tunnel: Wire connected to an undirected tunnel"** (`diag_c158_a3_2b_el.log:37-38`). That label names a loop's tunnel; a case tunnel appears as "Tunnel:" in this project's own record (`docs/d1/ring-p5.md:159`).
  - NI's description of the error is a tunnel with no source, or a source on both sides ([NI docs](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/errors/wire-connected-to-undirected-tunnel.html)). Loop 1.7's out-tunnels **#5204 and #10404** fit that description. Their source was #376 inside the loop, and their outer-terminal identities were lost through a change of direction (`diag_c158_p5s02a_scratch.log:277`).
  - Those two tunnels are re-fed by the K3/K4 groups (`w_k3f` / `w_k4f`, `plan_ring_p5s02r_in.json:843-948`), not by actions 24–40, which the claim names.
- **The claim leaves out the plan's own list of rows the move opened.** That list is `#2048` array/length and `#6384` actual # data points, error in and file # to append (`plan_ring_p5s02a.json:108-147`). The single "input of While Loop connected to an input of SubVI" item (a wire that joins two inputs and has no source) most plausibly sits on #5204 → #6384. That is my reading of the message, not something I found documented.

So the story was assembled after the result was seen, and the parts that can be checked don't line up.

## 2. Alternative explanation of the same evidence

The 11-item change is a **net** count per error class, not 11 identified items. For example, "not connected" is +1 and "loose ends" is +2.

- Step 1 **closed** at least one base row: `current frame data array in` was open in P5-1 and is wired by `w_el7_376`.
- The real graph also lost 12 (source, sink) pairs (`scratch.log:271`).

So "+2 loose ends" could just as well be +3 new and −1 closed. One of those new items could be a remnant of the move that **no later action re-feeds**, for example a branch of an output wire from #376 to a sink other than terminals 24139, 24235, 8776 or 24342. Under rule 1a that would be a real change to what the VI computes, not a by-design gap.

The checks that passed don't exclude this:
- **The graph-vs-simulation check (E1) doesn't cover leftover wires.** E1 reports "real graph == simulated step" (`scratch.log:268`). Yet the next check (D) records a source-only stub wire, 24091, that exists **only in the simulation** (`scratch.log:271-272`), and #25223's inner terminal is unwired in the real file (`:277-278`). E1's "diff 0" therefore can't vouch for the leftover-wire state that the Error List is reporting.
- **The Error List checker had no way to call anything by-design.** It ran with `plan None ... 0 derived licences` (`diag_c158_a3_2b_el.log:6`). With an explicit expected file, it drops plan-derived licences anyway (`tools/errorlist_check.py:225-228`).
- **No item has an object ID.** Every item's ID lookup reads "none: needs a Selection List[] 6349400 op (unbuilt)". Item-level attribution has never been measured.

**The prediction was also wrong in the other direction.** It expected +2 "input" items (the Dequeue `error in` and the Release inputs), and that class stayed at 1. This fits the known over-count of optional inputs (`ring-p5.md:163`), but it means "the 47 was an under-count" is not the whole picture.

## 3. Observation that would falsify the claim

Any item of a **new** class (items 1, 2, 4, 6 and 50 "has no source"; 7 and 8 "undirected tunnel"; 49 "input to input") is highlighted on an object that is **not** one of these:
- RSR #24083 or #24150 (inner side),
- tunnel #5204 or #10404,
- a wire whose sinks are all among the 8 rows the plan says the move opened (`#2048` ×2, `#376` ×3, `#6384` ×3).

Later, at the step-2 Error List: any of these classes left above zero once actions 24..80 are applied.

## 4. Cheapest discriminating test (offline, no LabVIEW)

1. **Open the screenshots already taken after each item was double-clicked.** For the 8 new-class items they are `tools/bench/errorlist_shots/bd_*_after{1,2,4,6,7,8,49,50}.png`. Check what is highlighted in each against the positions of #24083, #24150, #5204, #10404, #6384 and #2048 in `graph_ring_p5s01_20261009_125622.json`.
   - If all 8 are on those objects, the core of the claim survives, with the list corrected: K3/K4 instead of A48, and the case tunnels as the targets rather than the causes.
   - If even one is elsewhere, the claim is falsified and it becomes a rule-1a question.
2. The +1 "not connected" and +2 "loose ends" items can't be attributed by count, because the same classes already exist in the base file. Either diff each of those items' screenshots against the P5-1 run's screenshots, or say plainly that these 3 are **unattributed**.

**Before writing the expected file from these 56 items:** freezing 5 "has no source" items into it means a later real fault with the same wording would be absorbed silently. That is exactly why derived licences were dropped (`errorlist_check.py:225-227, 344-347`). Record them as unattributed until step 1 above has been run.

**What would change my mind:** step 1 putting all 8 new-class items on owed objects, plus a closed-vs-new account of the 3 net same-class items.

